  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x & >2x-3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x-2x & >2x-3-2x \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $5{x}^{2}>-3$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+3>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=5{x}^{2}+3.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $5{x}^{2}+3$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/60aede2dc3b2823aecd1e808210f96ffe5c6430d09a391fe6f4cf0e88a96e786.png)",
